WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:04.350
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:11.210
Earlier we had an episode about OpenAI's absolutely
amazing algorithm that mastered DOTA 2, a

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:16.160
competitive online battle arena game, and
managed to beat some of the best players in

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:19.990
the world in a relatively limited 1 versus
1 game mode.

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:24.480
While the full 5 versus 5 version of this
learning algorithm is still in the works,

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:30.230
scientists at OpenAI experimented with some
self-play in other games and have found some

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:31.640
remarkable results.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:36.750
You can see here that most of these amusing
experiments take place in a made up 3D game

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:38.300
with simulated physics.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:44.300
For instance, performing well with these humanoid
creatures means controlling 17 actuated joints

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:45.310
properly.

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:50.170
These agents use a reinforcement learning
algorithm to maximize a reward, for instance,

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:54.890
a sumo warrior gets a thousand points for
pushing their opponent out of the ring.

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:59.580
The first interesting thing is that a learning
curriculum was used, which means that the

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:04.559
algorithm was allowed to explore on their
own by relaxing the strict scores that are

00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:06.370
given only when winning.

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:10.840
This is combined with the fact that these
agents play against themselves led to some

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:12.490
remarkable emergent behaviors.

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:17.150
Here you can see with the score how much of
a difference this curriculum makes, and you

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:22.071
also see that whenever a plot is symmetric
that means that they are zero-sum games, so

00:01:22.071 --> 00:01:26.610
if one agent wins a given number of points,
the other loses the same amount.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:32.450
The self-play part is also particularly interesting
as many agents are being trained in parallel

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:37.350
at the same time, and if we're talking about
one versus one games, we have to create some

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:40.750
useful logic to decide who to pair with whom.

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:45.280
It seems that training against an older version
of a previously challenged opponent was the

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:46.600
best strategy.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:51.061
This makes sense because they are running
a similar algorithm, and for self-play, this

00:01:51.061 --> 00:01:55.390
means that the algorithm is asked to defeat
an older version of itself.

00:01:55.390 --> 00:02:00.159
If it can reliably do that, it will lead to
a smooth and predictable learning process.

00:02:00.159 --> 00:02:05.020
It is kinda incredible to think about the
fact that we have a virtual world with a bunch

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:10.640
of simulated learning creatures and we are
omnipotent beings trying to craft the optimal

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:12.450
learning experience for them.

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:15.420
The perks of being a researcher in machine
learning.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:17.380
And we are even being paid for this!

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:18.459
Isn't this incredible?

00:02:18.459 --> 00:02:21.560
Psst, don't tell anyone about this.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:25.170
There are so many interesting results here
and so much to talk about.

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:29.560
For instance, we haven't even talked about
transfer learning, where these creatures learn

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:34.920
to generalize their knowledge learned from
previous tasks to tackle new challenges more

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:35.920
efficiently.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:39.670
Make sure to have a look at the paper, and
the source code is available for everyone

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:40.760
free of charge.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:44.240
If you are one of those fellow tinkerers,
you'll be more than happy to look into the

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:45.470
video description.

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:50.220
If you wish to hear more about transfer learning,
subscribe and turn on notifications because

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:54.380
the next episode is going to about some really
cool results in this area.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.400
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

